@fm description | description | The words used by the Crumbwick Bakery crew and what each one means.
# Glossary
s description | New crew members hear these words on their first morning.
t none | Word | none | Meaning
t description | Pinnock | description | A small round dough ball that is the start of a loaf
t description | Sconce room | description | The small cupboard behind the till that holds the keys
t description | Plinth | description | The steel bench under the east window where racks cool
t description | Wobbin | description | The name of the sourdough starter
t description | Tarn flour | description | The coarse flour from the hill mill
## Usage
s rule | Use the word pinnock for the dough ball and never for a finished loaf.
s prohibition | Do not call the sconce room the safe, because it holds keys and not money.
s example | For example, write that the opening crew carried six pinnocks down, not six loaves.
s description | The word plinth comes from the old stone bench that once stood there.
s rule | Ask if you hear a word that is not here.
## Short forms
l description | OB means the order book
l description | CB means the closing book
l description | SB means the shift book
l rule | Spell out a short form the first time you use it in a note
l prohibition | Do not use a short form on a customer label
## Naming loaves
s gate{gloss_names} | Give a new loaf a name of one or two words, and tell the head baker before you use it.
s gate | Check the name against the allergen folder before it goes on a tag.
s description | The best known loaf is the Tarn Rye, which sells out by ten.
s example | Case: a loaf called Nut Free Rye was named by a new crew member and had to be renamed the same day.
